            "review_title": "Review at Hibiki BBQ Thit Wagyu Nuong Hibiki",
            "review_text": "maryyeo\nHo Chi Minh City, Vietnam4531 contributions\n0\nAuthentic, unpretentious, good service, good quality. Can't wait to be back.\nJun 2020\nBeen visiting several Wagyu restaurants around town because the hubby has a serious craving for authentic Wagyu - Hibiki, Yazawa, Caminu, Ibuki, SOL butcher, etc and we have to say, Hibiki is probably our favourite for now.\n\nTheir cuts of meats are fresh, awesomely marbled, AMAZINGLY clear presentation with naming of each beef type in Japanese+English+PICTORIAL format, and the charcoal grill concept is done so well that we don't feel the heat nor see any flying ashes at all. Love the service staff for being attentive, yet non-intrusive, knowledgeable about the menu, soft-spoken and can communicate well in English. \n\nWe arrived at around 6.30pm on a Friday evening and we had the whole lower floor to ourselves for a while. Highly recommend you to come early. Most customers here are Japanese and most arrived after 7pm. The louder, rowdier crowd were seated upstairs (not sure if there were private rooms upstairs) and I found that it was thoughtful of the staff to let the quieter customers enjoy the peace downstairs.\n\nSure, the meals here ain't cheap if you compare it to average meal prices in Vietnam, but comparing the prices here to other Wagyu restaurants, the sets/combos here are of pretty good value. We ordered the Hibikimori and Omakase Wagyumori sets at 1,480,000vnd and 750,000vnd respectively and shared a refreshing chilled noodles (absolutely recommend this if you wanna give your tongue a 'breather' between tasting each amazing cut of beef, kinda like how a waft of coffee beans gently resets your sense of smell in between perfumes). We were stuffed and the price was still below 3 million VND, which is much cheaper WITH better quality than some other fine-dining Wagyu restaurants we've been to.\n\nAuthentic, unpretentious, good service, good quality. Can't wait to be back.\nRead more\nValue\nService\nFood\nWritten October 9, 2020",
